"I have made firm its pillars." After proclaiming and testifying to sinners the things foretold, and after teaching everyone the word concerning the judgment, what [?]? "I will sing forever to the God of Jacob." For I know as my own Father the one who became the God of that man beloved of God, and to him I will sing, and to him I will report my own achievements. Then, having thus received from the Father the authority to judge, "I will break all the horns of sinners." I allowed them to be exalted for the time during which forbearance, patience and kindness had to be shown; but at the end I will break their horns, which were lifted up against God. Conversely, I will exalt the horns of the righteous, since they had been humbled. They will be exalted when he reigns together with his own King, according to the Apostle, who says: "Christ the firstfruits; then those who belong to Christ at his coming; then comes the end, when he hands over the kingdom to God the Father, when he has abolished all authority and power." Then too the righteous will drink the cup of immortal life, which was promised to them when he said: "until I drink it new with you in the kingdom of heaven."

FOR THE END, IN [?], A PSALM OF [?]

"God is known in Judea; his name is great in Israel." The present psalm too is one of those that point toward a good end. It agrees with the one before it, since it contains words about the judgment of God. This it shows when it goes on to say: "From heaven you made judgment heard; the earth feared and was still when God arose to judgment, to save all the meek of the earth." In that psalm it was said to the lawless: "Do not lift up your horn, and do not raise your horn on high"; but in the one before us it says: "The foolish in heart were troubled; they slept their sleep, and all the men of wealth found nothing." These were the ones who were lifting up their horns. Through these words the text teaches a very deep doctrine, so that we may know who the true Israel is, and what its country is, the one that is truly named Judea; what the true Jerusalem is, and what sort of place God's dwelling is. These things the prophetic Spirit arranges with surpassing wisdom: at one and the same time it gives no offense [?] to those who hear in a lower and more Jewish way, thanks to the shared names of the things it signifies, and it teaches the contemplative the lessons of divine doctrine. The Jew, then, being nothing more than flesh and blood, will hear these words in a fleshly and bodily way, and will then be puffed up, vainly inflated by the mind of his flesh and not holding fast to the head, as the holy Apostle says. He will therefore suppose that he himself is Israel, that Judea is nothing other than the country of the Palestinians, and that the place of God is the earthly Jerusalem, since the Hebrew wording reads that his place has come to be in Salem;
